  { q: 'What did ravens symbolise to the Celts?', a: 'To the Celts (particularly the Irish and Welsh), the raven symbolised war, death, prophecy, and sovereignty. Ravens were the birds of the battlefield — real ravens fed on the slain — and this real-world presence was invested with supernatural meaning. The raven was the form taken by battle goddesses (the Mórrígan, Badb, Nemain in Irish tradition) who shaped the outcome of fights and gathered the dead. Ravens were also associated with kingship through Bran the Blessed (whose name means "raven/crow" in Welsh) and with prophetic wisdom in multiple Celtic traditions.' },
  { q: 'Who is the Celtic raven goddess?', a: 'The primary Celtic raven goddess is the Mórrígan — an Irish sovereignty and battle goddess who appears in raven form throughout the Ulster Cycle. She prophesies battle outcomes, transforms into a crow or raven to fly over battlefields, and harries warriors she opposes. The Mórrígan is part of a triad of Irish war goddesses: she is associated with Badb (specifically identified with the battle-crow/raven) and Nemain (panic and battle-fury). These three aspects of the battle goddess are all associated with corvid form.' },
  { q: 'What is the connection between ravens and Celtic kingship?', a: 'The connection between ravens and Celtic kingship operates through several interlocking themes: the raven as the battle-goddess\'s bird (victory in battle = legitimate kingship); the raven as a prophetic bird whose calls foretold the outcome of wars affecting kings; and the figure of Bran the Blessed (Welsh: "raven/crow"), whose severed head continued to prophesy after death and whose ravens are still believed, in English folklore, to protect the Tower of London. This link between the raven, prophecy, death, and legitimate sovereignty is a consistent feature of Celtic warrior kingship.' },
